[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
describe three different problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one. Each problem
now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser will not close #4471. Reports for the other two problems keep coming after the fix ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/mosk88/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/mosk88/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2',
    shift: 'https://github.com/mosk88/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3',
  }}
/>

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day before the month. The shared parser reads every date month
first, so 7 April becomes 4 July. It reaches every tenant through every channel: app, email and
form. The queued fix is for this problem.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type the month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The form then reads the date the
tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint tells them, so their dates come out flipped.
It only reaches tenants on booking-v3, and only through the form. The reports look the same as
the parser's (day and month swapped), but the fix is in the form's hint, not the parser.

Intake cannot tell the two apart. It tags from the reporter's browser, so it tags `hint` for an
`en-US` browser and `parser` otherwise. The tenant decides the cause, not the browser.

### `shift`: the date moves by one day

These reports are not flipped dates. Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May. A meeting in Lisbon
lands on Tuesday and the same meeting in Toronto on Monday. An invite arrives before its booking.
This points to a time zone being dropped or applied twice. The desk read these reports as a
one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but still filed them under the parser by tenant, and the desk
counts them there too.

## How each report is placed

`causes.ts` places each report by its tenant: the locale they write dates in, and the booking form
they run. The desk's one-day-move notes add the `shift` problem on top. Here is the same data as
the desk's cause table shows it:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why it was one ticket

The desk merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). All
three problems produce the same subjects: "Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong". So subject
matching put them in one ticket, and nobody read the bodies. Decision 0001 needs a new record
that merges by cause.

